{"task_id": "HumanEval/31", "solution": "```python\ndef is_prime(n):\n    \"\"\"Return true if a given number is prime, and false otherwise.\n    >>> is_prime(6)\n    False\n    >>> is_prime(101)\n    True\n    >>> is_prime(11)\n    True\n    >>> is_prime(13441)\n    True\n    >>> is_prime(61)\n    True\n    >>> is_prime(4)\n    False\n    >>> is_prime(1)\n    False\n    \"\"\"\n    if n <= 1:\n        return False\n    if n <= 3:\n        return True\n    if n % 2 == 0 or n % 3 == 0:\n        return False\n    i = 5\n    while i * i <= n:\n        if n % i == 0 or n % (i + 2) == 0:\n            return False\n        i += 6\n    return True\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/33", "solution": "```python\ndef sort_third(l: list):\n    \"\"\"This function takes a list l and returns a list l' such that\n    l' is identical to l in the indicies that are not divisible by three, while its values at the indicies that are divisible by three are equal\n    to the values of the corresponding indicies of l, but sorted.\n    >>> sort_third([1, 2, 3])\n    [1, 2, 3]\n    >>> sort_third([5, 6, 3, 4, 8, 9, 2])\n    [2, 6, 3, 4, 8, 9, 5]\n    \"\"\"\n\n    # Identify indices divisible by three and extract their corresponding values.\n    third_divisible_values = []\n    third_divisible_indices = []\n    for i, val in enumerate(l):\n        if i % 3 == 0:\n            third_divisible_values.append(val)\n            third_divisible_indices.append(i)\n\n    # Sort the collected values in ascending order.\n    third_divisible_values.sort()\n\n    # Create a new list, copying the original to preserve non-targeted elements.\n    result_list = list(l)\n\n    # Place the sorted values back into the new list at their original indices.\n    for i, original_index in enumerate(third_divisible_indices):\n        result_list[original_index] = third_divisible_values[i]\n\n    return result_list\n```"}
