{"task_id": "HumanEval/69", "solution": "```python\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef search(lst):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the greatest integer X in a list such that its frequency is >= X.\n    Returns -1 if no such integer exists.\n    \"\"\"\n    counts = Counter(lst)\n\n    # Sort unique numbers in descending order to find the greatest first.\n    unique_numbers = sorted(counts.keys(), reverse=True)\n\n    for num in unique_numbers:\n        # Check if the number's frequency meets the criterion.\n        if counts[num] >= num:\n            return num\n\n    return -1\n```\n"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/68", "solution": "```python\ndef pluck(arr):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the smallest even-valued node and its index from an array of non-negative integers.\n    Returns `[value, index]`, or `[]` if no even values are found or the array is empty.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not arr:\n        return []\n\n    smallest_even_value = None\n    smallest_even_index = None\n\n    for index, value in enumerate(arr):\n        if value % 2 == 0:  # Check if the value is even\n            if smallest_even_value is None:\n                smallest_even_value = value\n                smallest_even_index = index\n            elif value < smallest_even_value:\n                smallest_even_value = value\n                smallest_even_index = index\n            elif value == smallest_even_value and index < smallest_even_index:\n                smallest_even_index = index\n\n    if smallest_even_value is not None:\n        return [smallest_even_value, smallest_even_index]\n    else:\n        return []\n```\n"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/67", "solution": "```python\nimport re\n\ndef fruit_distribution(s, n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the number of mangoes given apple/orange counts and total fruits.\n    Assumes valid input string format and total fruit count based on critical requirements.\n    \"\"\"\n    match = re.match(r\"(\\d+)\\s*apples\\s*and\\s*(\\d+)\\s*oranges\", s)\n\n    apples = int(match.group(1))\n    oranges = int(match.group(2))\n\n    mangoes = n - (apples + oranges)\n\n    return mangoes\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/68", "solution": "```python\ndef pluck(arr):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the smallest even non-negative integer and its index in the input list.\n    Returns [value, index] or an empty list if no even number is found or array is empty.\n    \"\"\"\n    smallest_even_value = float('inf')\n    smallest_even_index = -1\n\n    if not arr:\n        return []\n\n    for index, value in enumerate(arr):\n        if value % 2 == 0:  # Check if the value is even.\n            if value < smallest_even_value:\n                smallest_even_value = value\n                smallest_even_index = index\n            elif value == smallest_even_value and index < smallest_even_index:\n                # This ensures the smallest index for ties, though forward iteration often covers this.\n                smallest_even_index = index\n\n    if smallest_even_index != -1:\n        return [smallest_even_value, smallest_even_index]\n    else:\n        return []\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/69", "solution": "```python\nimport collections\n\ndef search(lst):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the greatest integer in a list whose frequency is at least its value.\n    Returns -1 if no such integer exists.\n    \"\"\"\n    frequencies = collections.Counter(lst)\n\n    qualifying_integers = []\n    for num, freq in frequencies.items():\n        if freq >= num:\n            qualifying_integers.append(num)\n\n    if not qualifying_integers:\n        return -1\n\n    return max(qualifying_integers)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/70", "solution": "```python\ndef strange_sort_list(lst):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a list of integers, return a new list sorted in \"strange order\":\n    minimum value, then maximum of the remaining, then minimum, and so on.\n    Examples:\n    strange_sort_list([1, 2, 3, 4]) == [1, 4, 2, 3]\n    strange_sort_list([5, 5, 5, 5]) == [5, 5, 5, 5]\n    strange_sort_list([]) == []\n    \"\"\"\n    if not lst:\n        return []\n\n    # Create a sorted copy to easily pick min (from start) and max (from end)\n    working_list = sorted(lst)\n    result = []\n\n    while working_list:\n        # Take minimum\n        result.append(working_list.pop(0))\n        if not working_list:\n            break\n        # Take maximum of remaining\n        result.append(working_list.pop())\n\n    return result\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/70", "solution": "```python\ndef strange_sort_list(lst):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a list of integers, return a new list sorted in a strange min-max alternating order.\n    The sorting starts with the minimum value, then the maximum of the remaining integers, and so on.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not lst:\n        return []\n\n    working_list = sorted(lst)\n    result_list = []\n    is_min_turn = True\n\n    while working_list:\n        if is_min_turn:\n            result_list.append(working_list.pop(0))\n        else:\n            result_list.append(working_list.pop(-1))\n        is_min_turn = not is_min_turn\n\n    return result_list\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/71", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef triangle_area(a, b, c):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given three side lengths, calculates the area of a triangle.\n    Returns the area rounded to 2 decimal places, or -1 if invalid.\n    \"\"\"\n    INVALID_TRIANGLE_RETURN_VALUE = -1.0\n    DECIMAL_PLACES_FOR_ROUNDING = 2\n\n    # Validate if all sides are positive.\n    if not (a > 0 and b > 0 and c > 0):\n        return INVALID_TRIANGLE_RETURN_VALUE\n\n    # Validate the triangle inequality theorem.\n    if not ((a + b > c) and (a + c > b) and (b + c > a)):\n        return INVALID_TRIANGLE_RETURN_VALUE\n\n    # Calculate the semi-perimeter.\n    s = (a + b + c) / 2\n\n    # Calculate the area using Heron's formula.\n    area = math.sqrt(s * (s - a) * (s - b) * (s - c))\n\n    # Round the calculated area to two decimal places.\n    return round(area, DECIMAL_PLACES_FOR_ROUNDING)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/73", "solution": "```python\ndef smallest_change(arr):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the minimum number of elements to change to make an array palindromic.\n    It counts mismatches between elements at symmetric positions.\n    \"\"\"\n    changes = 0\n    left = 0\n    right = len(arr) - 1\n\n    while left < right:\n        if arr[left] != arr[right]:\n            changes += 1\n        left += 1\n        right -= 1\n    return changes\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/71", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef triangle_area(a, b, c):\n    '''\n    Given the lengths of the three sides of a triangle, return its area rounded to 2 decimal places.\n    Returns -1 if the sides do not form a valid triangle.\n    '''\n    # Validate that all side lengths are positive.\n    if not (a > 0 and b > 0 and c > 0):\n        return -1\n\n    # Validate the triangle inequality theorem.\n    if not (a + b > c and a + c > b and b + c > a):\n        return -1\n\n    # Calculate the semi-perimeter.\n    s = (a + b + c) / 2\n\n    # Calculate the area using Heron's formula.\n    area = math.sqrt(s * (s - a) * (s - b) * (s - c))\n\n    # Round the calculated area to two decimal places.\n    return round(area, 2)\n```"}
