{"task_id": "HumanEval/136", "solution": "```python\ndef largest_smallest_integers(lst):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the largest negative and smallest positive integers in a list.\n    Returns (None, None) for empty lists or lists with only zero, or None for either if not found.\n    \"\"\"\n    largest_negative = None\n    smallest_positive = None\n\n    for num in lst:\n        if num < 0:\n            if largest_negative is None or num > largest_negative:\n                largest_negative = num\n        elif num > 0:\n            if smallest_positive is None or num < smallest_positive:\n                smallest_positive = num\n\n    return (largest_negative, smallest_positive)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/132", "solution": "```python\ndef is_nested(string):\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if the input string contains a nested bracket subsequence '[[...]]'.\n    It finds if there exist indices i < j < k < l such that s[i], s[j] are '[' and s[k], s[l] are ']'.\n    \"\"\"\n    idx1 = -1\n    idx2 = -1\n    idx3 = -1\n    idx4 = -1\n\n    # Find the first '['\n    idx1 = string.find('[')\n    if idx1 == -1:\n        return False\n\n    # Find the second '[' after idx1\n    idx2 = string.find('[', idx1 + 1)\n    if idx2 == -1:\n        return False\n\n    # Find the first ']' after idx2\n    idx3 = string.find(']', idx2 + 1)\n    if idx3 == -1:\n        return False\n\n    # Find the second ']' after idx3\n    idx4 = string.find(']', idx3 + 1)\n    if idx4 == -1:\n        return False\n\n    return True\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/135", "solution": "```python\ndef can_arrange(arr):\n    \"\"\"Returns the largest index of an element which is smaller than its predecessor.\n    If no such element exists, returns -1.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(arr)\n    if n < 2:\n        return -1\n\n    for i in range(n - 1, 0, -1):\n        if arr[i] < arr[i-1]:\n            return i\n\n    return -1\n```"}
